IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Canada Pension Plan Act (R.S.C. 1985, c. C-8) and Canada Pension Plan Regulations (C.R.C., c. 385)
-- THE CORE: the vocabulary that two or more pieces of the encoding share.
--
-- This module declares types and one date constructor. It has no rules of its own.
-- The rules that go with these types are in cpp-core-calendar.l4 (periods and months),
-- cpp-core-definitions.l4 (section 2(1)), cpp-core-age.l4 (section 2(2)) and
-- cpp-core-application.l4 (sections 3 to 4.1). NOTES.md is the guide to all of them.
--
-- Conventions the types rely on (the full list is in NOTES.md, "Naming conventions"):
--   money    a NUMBER, in Canadian dollars, cents as decimals
--   rates    a NUMBER that is a fraction: 5.95% is written 5.95% and is 0.0595
--   a year   a NUMBER, the calendar year (section 2(1) "year")
--   a month  the DATE of its first day, written YMD y m 1
--   an age   a NUMBER of whole years
--   a date   a DATE, written YMD year month day
--
-- The text rendering this encoding reads is an unofficial converter rendering of the
-- Justice Laws consolidation; nothing in this folder has been checked against the official text.

§ `Canada Pension Plan -- shared vocabulary`

§§ `Dates`

-- The stable prelude has no YMD. This is the shim the brief asks for.
-- Nothing else in the folder may define YMD: a name defined in two imported modules is unusable.
-- Date d m y rolls over (Date 31 2 2026 is 3 March 2026), so YMD does not refuse an impossible date.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YearMonthDay y m d MEANS Date d m y -- renamed from YMD so it cannot collide with the YMD in newer daydate libraries; see docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md section 5

§§ `The three tiers of contribution and of pensionable earnings`

-- The Act runs three parallel regimes side by side: the base contribution, and from 2019 and 2024
-- the first and the second additional contribution. Almost every defined term of Parts I and II
-- comes in all three tiers (contribution, contribution rate, contributory period, monthly
-- pensionable earnings, unadjusted pensionable earnings, total pensionable earnings ...).
-- A rule that is the same in all three takes a Tier; a rule that differs by tier is written per tier.
-- src:25 | base contribution means a contribution under subsection 8(1), 9(1) or 10(1); (cotisation de base)
-- src:45 | first additional contribution means a contribution under subsection 8(1.1), 9(1.1) or 10(1.1); (première cotisation supplémentaire)
-- src:67 | second additional contribution means a contribution under subsection 8(1.2), 9(1.2) or 10(1.2); (deuxième cotisation supplémentaire)
@ref Act s 2(1) "base contribution", "first additional contribution", "second additional contribution" (src:25-67)
DECLARE Tier IS ONE OF
    `the base tier`
    `the first additional tier`
    `the second additional tier`

§§ `The three capacities in which a contribution is made`

-- Sections 8, 9 and 10 impose the contribution on an employee, on an employer and on a self-employed
-- person; section 11.1 sets "the contribution rate for that employee, employer and self-employed person".
-- src:34 | contribution rate, in respect of an employee, an employer and a self-employed person for a year, means the contribution rate for that employee, employer and self-employed person for the year determined in accordance with section 11.1; (taux de cotisation)
@ref Act s 2(1) "contribution rate" (src:34)
DECLARE `Contributing capacity` IS ONE OF
    `contributing as an employee`
    `contributing as an employer`
    `contributing as a self-employed person`

§§ `The eight benefits`

-- The benefits the Act pays, in the order of section 44(1)(a) to (h). Part II (cp3, cp4, cp5), the
-- Regulations' Part V (cr2) and the Act's Part III all name them.
-- src:973 | (a) a retirement pension shall be paid to a contributor who has reached sixty years of age;
-- src:974 | (b) a disability pension shall be paid to a contributor who has not reached sixty-five years of age, to whom no retirement pension is payable, who is disabled and who
-- src:979 | (c) a death benefit shall be paid to the estate or succession of a deceased contributor who has made base contributions for not less than the minimum qualifying period;
-- src:980 | (d) a survivor’s pension shall be paid
-- src:988 | (e) a disabled contributor’s child’s benefit shall be paid to each child of a disabled contributor who
-- src:993 | (f) an orphan’s benefit shall be paid to each orphan of a deceased contributor who has made base contributions for not less than the minimum qualifying period;
-- src:994 | (g) a post-retirement benefit shall be paid to a beneficiary of a retirement pension under this Act or under a provincial pension plan who has made a contribution in respect of the post-r...
-- src:995 | (h) a post-retirement disability benefit shall be paid to a beneficiary of a retirement pension who has not reached 65 years of age and is disabled if
@ref Act s 44(1)(a) to (h) (src:973-995)
DECLARE `Kind of benefit` IS ONE OF
    `a retirement pension`
    `a disability pension`
    `a death benefit`
    `a survivor's pension`
    `a disabled contributor's child's benefit`
    `an orphan's benefit`
    `a post-retirement benefit`
    `a post-retirement disability benefit`

§§ `The two Ministers`

-- The Act names two Ministers: the Minister of National Revenue for Part I (and for section 4.1),
-- and "the Minister" of Employment and Social Development for Parts II and III.
-- src:124 | 5 (1) In this Part, Minister means the Minister of National Revenue.
-- src:945 | Minister means the Minister of Employment and Social Development; (ministre)
@ref Act s 5(1), s 42(1) "Minister" (src:124), (src:945)
DECLARE Minister IS ONE OF
    `the Minister of National Revenue`
    `the Minister of Employment and Social Development`

§§ `A time limit`

-- Every time limit the Act sets is a number of days, of months or of years counted from a date or
-- back from a date: "6 months after the day of death", "a continuous period of at least one year",
-- "not later than twelve months before the first day of the third year ...".
-- The calendar module (cpp-core-calendar.l4) turns a Time limit and a date into the date it ends.
-- src:23 | (a) where the person died after October in the year and before May in the immediately following year, the day that is 6 months after the day of death, and
-- src:31 | common-law partner, in relation to a contributor, means a person who is cohabiting with the contributor in a conjugal relationship at the relevant time, having so cohabited with the contributor for a continuous period of at least one year. For greater certainty, in the case of a contributor’s death, the relevant time means the time of the contributor’s death; (conjoint de fait)
@ref Act s 2(1) "balance-due day", "common-law partner" (src:23), (src:31)
DECLARE `Time limit` IS ONE OF
    `a number of days` HAS `number of days` IS A NUMBER
    `a number of months` HAS `number of months` IS A NUMBER
    `a number of years` HAS `number of years` IS A NUMBER

§§ `A province, and whether it provides a comprehensive pension plan`

-- The Act never names a province. Whether a province is "a province providing a comprehensive
-- pension plan" turns on a regulation that is not among the texts this encoding reads, so the
-- status is a fact about the province that the caller supplies (see NOTES.md, fork register).
-- src:92 | province providing a comprehensive pension plan means a province prescribed by a regulation made on the recommendation of the Minister of Employment and Social Development for the purposes of this Act as a province
@ref Act s 3(1) "province providing a comprehensive pension plan" (src:92)
DECLARE Province HAS
    `name of the province` IS A STRING
    `a province providing a comprehensive pension plan` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Unadjusted pensionable earnings`

-- The Act's one collective noun for the three tiers of earnings that the Record of Earnings keeps
-- and that benefits are calculated from. Sections 53 to 53.2 work out the three amounts, and sections
-- 55 to 55.2 divide them between spouses or common-law partners.
-- src:79 | unadjusted pensionable earnings means base unadjusted pensionable earnings, first additional unadjusted pensionable earnings and second additional unadjusted pensionable earnings; (gains non ajustés ouvrant droit à pension)
@ref Act s 2(1) "unadjusted pensionable earnings" (src:79)
DECLARE `Unadjusted pensionable earnings` HAS
    `base unadjusted pensionable earnings` IS A NUMBER
    `first additional unadjusted pensionable earnings` IS A NUMBER
    `second additional unadjusted pensionable earnings` IS A NUMBER
